<article class="env-proposition" id="06RM">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06RM">Proposition <span data-tag="06RM">4.7.6.15</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category, let $n \geq 0$ be an integer, and let $\operatorname{cosk}_{n}(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$ denote the $n$-coskeleton of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ (see Notation <a data-tag="052H" href="/tag/052H">3.5.3.18</a>). Then $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is locally $(n-2)$-truncated if and only if the tautological map $\theta : \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\rightarrow \operatorname{cosk}_{n}(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$ is a trivial Kan fibration. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      By definition, the morphism $\theta $ is a trivial Kan fibration if and only if every lifting problem 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ \operatorname{\partial \Delta }^ m \ar [r] \ar [d] &amp;  \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\ar [d] \\ \Delta ^{m} \ar@ {--&gt;}[ur] \ar [r] &amp;  \operatorname{cosk}_{n}(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) } \]
</div>
<p>
       admits a solution. For $m \leq n$ this condition is vacuous, and for $m &gt; n$ it is a reformulation of the criterion of Remark <a data-tag="06RL" href="/tag/06RL">4.7.6.11</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</p></p></article>
